    "original_post_text": "My Two Cents on The Lodge Situation \u2014 Just a Theory, But Hear Me Out\n\nLet me be clear upfront, I don't know Doug Polk personally. Never met the man. So I'm reserving full judgment on the current investigation until the facts come out. No arrests. No charges. Just an ongoing investigation at this point.\nBut here's what I DO know.\n\nWhen Doug Polk came to Texas, he didn't just come to run a poker room. He came with ambition and he put his money where his mouth was. He and his partners, Andrew Neeme and Brad Owen, acquired majority ownership of The Lodge Card Club in Round Rock in 2022, building it into the largest poker room in Texas. Then in 2024 they purchased Rounders Card Club in San Antonio, rebranding it as The Lodge Card Club San Antonio and expanding their footprint even further.\n\nBut beyond the business moves, Doug became something Texas poker didn't really have before, a legitimate, organized political voice.\n\nHe helped launch Texans for Texas Hold'em, a lobbying group created to protect the social poker club model and push for legal clarity in Texas gambling law. He worked directly with state legislators on bills like HB 2345 to formally legalize membership poker clubs. He appeared before city councils, including in Farmers Branch right here in DFW, fighting for zoning rights so new rooms could open. He used his massive YouTube and social media platform to educate players, mobilize the community, and keep pressure on lawmakers. That's not nothing. That's real advocacy work.\n\nNow here's my theory, and I want to stress this is just a theory.\nTexas poker has been under attack long before Doug Polk ever showed up. I've been playing in this state since 2014 and the legal gray area has always been a weapon used against this community. The uncertainty isn't an accident, there are powerful interests who benefit from keeping Texas poker in limbo because it means they can shut you down at any moment.\n\nDoug Polk became the most visible, most vocal, most politically organized threat to that gray area. And now his room is the one being investigated by a financial crimes unit with the IRS involved.\nDo I think Doug, Brad Owen and Andrew Neeme are suddenly the Texas Poker Mafia? Naw. I don't buy that at all. Is it possible there are financial issues within the operation that need to be addressed? Sure, and if laws were broken, then handle it accordingly. We need poker rooms that set the right example.\n\nBut let's not lose sight of the bigger picture. Texas poker is under attack. It was under attack before Doug got here and it'll be under attack after this situation resolves. The people who want this industry gone are still out there and they are still organized.\n\nI hope for the sake of the employees, the players, and the future of Texas poker that The Lodge finds its way back to the felt in Austin. And to Doug, Brad, Andrew and the entire Lodge team, I wish you all the best of luck, because honestly this fight is far from over.\nIf you own a poker room in Texas right now, just know, you could be next. This isn't paranoia, it's the reality of operating in a state where the law still hasn't given this industry the protection it deserves. Stay sharp. Stay clean. Be safe out there.\n\nThis is Sham, aka ShamGod Poker,  signing off. I'm proud to be part of this Texas poker community and I'm looking forward to staying in this fight with all of you.\n\n#TexasPoker #TheLodge #ShamGodPoker #TexansForTexasHoldEm",
    "expanded_title": "My Two Cents on The Lodge Situation: Why the Gray Area Is the Real Weapon Against Texas Poker",
    "expanded_deck": "Doug Polk became the most visible, politically organized voice pushing for legal clarity in Texas poker \u2014 and that context matters when looking at what's happening in Round Rock.",
    "expanded_body": "Originally posted March 13, 2026.\n\nLet me be clear upfront: I don't know Doug Polk personally. Never met the man. So I'm reserving full judgment on the current investigation until the facts come out. No arrests. No charges. Just an ongoing investigation at this point.\n\nBut here's what I DO know.\n\nWhen Doug Polk came to Texas, he didn't just come to run a poker room. He came with ambition and he put his money where his mouth was. He and his partners, Andrew Neeme and Brad Owen, acquired majority ownership of The Lodge Card Club in Round Rock in 2022, building it into the largest poker room in Texas. Then in 2024 they purchased Rounders Card Club in San Antonio, rebranding it as The Lodge Card Club San Antonio and expanding their footprint even further.\n\nBut beyond the business moves, Doug became something Texas poker didn't really have before: a legitimate, organized political voice.\n\nHe helped launch Texans for Texas Hold'em, a lobbying group created to protect the social poker club model and push for legal clarity in Texas gambling law. He worked directly with state legislators on bills like HB 2345 to formally legalize membership poker clubs. He appeared before city councils, including in Farmers Branch right here in DFW, fighting for zoning rights so new rooms could open. He used his massive YouTube and social media platform to educate players, mobilize the community, and keep pressure on lawmakers. That's not nothing. That's real advocacy work.\n\nNow here's my theory \u2014 and I want to stress this is just a theory.\n\nTexas poker has been under attack long before Doug Polk ever showed up. I've been playing in this state since 2014, and the legal gray area has always been a weapon used against this community. The uncertainty isn't an accident; there are powerful interests who benefit from keeping Texas poker in limbo because it means they can shut you down at any moment.\n\nDoug Polk became the most visible, most vocal, most politically organized threat to that gray area. And now his room is the one being investigated by a financial crimes unit with the IRS involved.\n\nDo I think Doug, Brad Owen, and Andrew Neeme are suddenly the Texas Poker Mafia? Naw. I don't buy that at all. Is it possible there are financial issues within the operation that need to be addressed? Sure \u2014 and if laws were broken, then handle it accordingly. We need poker rooms that set the right example.\n\nBut let's not lose sight of the bigger picture. Texas poker is under attack. It was under attack before Doug got here, and it'll be under attack after this situation resolves. The people who want this industry gone are still out there and they are still organized.\n\nI hope for the sake of the employees, the players, and the future of Texas poker that The Lodge finds its way back to the felt in Austin. And to Doug, Brad, Andrew, and the entire Lodge team, I wish you all the best of luck, because honestly this fight is far from over.\n\nIf you own a poker room in Texas right now, just know: you could be next. This isn't paranoia \u2014 it's the reality of operating in a state where the law still hasn't given this industry the protection it deserves. Stay sharp. Stay clean. Be safe out there.\n\nThis is Sham, aka ShamGod Poker, signing off. I'm proud to be part of this Texas poker community, and I'm looking forward to staying in this fight with all of you.\n\n\u2014 Sham",
